[decoded] "The sky appears blue during the day due to a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight contains all colors of the rainbow, but blue light has a shorter wavelength and scatters much more easily when it hits gas molecules in the Earth's atmosphere than other colors. This scattered blue light reaches our eyes from all directions, making the sky look blue.\n\nAt sunset, the sun is lower on the horizon, so sunlight must travel through a much thicker layer of the atmosphere to reach us. During this longer journey, most of the blue light is scattered away completely. The remaining light, which has longer wavelengths like red, orange, and yellow, passes through with less scattering. Consequently, our eyes perceive the sun and the surrounding sky as warm, reddish hues."
